00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.230 align:start position:0%
 
meet dr vivek morthy he's the united  states surgeon general what does that  mean and what does he do the surgeon  general is sometimes called the nation's  doctor he or she is the government's  primary spokesperson  on all matters of public health i'm kind  of a big deal recently i was invited to  trek down to dc  and meet with dr morty to ask him some  of my most burning questions  surgeon general thank you so much for  joining us on the youtube channel we  very much appreciate your time we know  it's limited we know you're doing  some amazing things here at the white  house actually all across the united  states  because your position really covers all  of the us can you tell us a little bit  about that well absolutely and dr mike  it's so great to be with you today i'm  glad we're having this conversation  you know i'm privileged to serve as  sergeant general it's a job i was uh  fortunate to have before in the obama  administration and the job of the  surgeon general is really to provide  the country with the best possible  information based on science  so they can make good decisions about  their health and during my time mr  general one of my hopes is  that we can first and foremost you know  turn this pandemic around and that's why  i want to do everything i can  to make sure people know the facts about  the vaccine so they can get vaccinated  protect themselves and their families  but i also want us to focus and have a  conversation  about some of the other challenges that  we're facing that have been made worse  by covet  mental health substance use disorders  and more broadly challenges around  equity  everyone knows the position of surgeon  general for  its pop culture meaning of being on  labels tobacco products  alcohol products what's the next label  do we have one is it going to be social  media vaping is there one in the works  that perhaps the youtube community isn't  aware of yet well  there's no label in the works at the  moment but  there are things that that i'm worried  about that i think that we do need to  raise a flag about  in 2016 when our surgeon general we  actually issued  a report about e-cigarettes in you okay  recognizing that  you know for many uh children uh  millions in fact if kids are under that  this was becoming a way through which  they were  developing independence actually on  nicotine and uh  there are other areas also where parents  are increasingly raising concerns  to our office and others around for  example social media and  wondering what are the impacts on the  mental health of my children and i  should just say lastly you know  when it comes to technology platforms  there's also a profound challenge we  have with misinformation  right now we see that in rampant with  covet  throughout this pandemic particularly  now with the vaccines  and so one question that you know we've  got to ask is what's your responsibility  to make sure  that there's accurate information that  users are getting how do you  see yourself playing a role as surgeon  general to guide this  conversation movement towards taking  mental health seriously  talking about it responsibly what can we  do from a national perspective here on  this  well it's such an important question and  i think we actually have to tackle it on  several fronts because we have actually  even pre-pandemic  been really challenged in the field of  mental health we've struggled with  access to mental health care as you know  in terms of mental health workforce  we just don't have enough people to  provide good care but we're also not  doing enough when it comes to prevention  there are programs that we could invest  in right now  in schools across our country that would  help build a foundation  uh for strong mental health and  emotional well-being in our kids but  we're just not  investing there but finally on a  cultural level we have to change how we  think as a country as a society about  mental health  we have to see it as an equal part of  health but also not as something  to be ashamed of if we're struggling  with our mental health i've also  read and heard that you have a strong  passion for advocating for the mental  health of providers  nurses doctors pharmacists lab techs  all the people who've been on the front  lines throughout this pandemic  what can we do to help those individuals  because like right now  obviously there are improvements being  made there's decreased hospitalizations  i have a few er colleagues who told me  over their last three four shifts they  haven't seen a single covet patient  which is amazing what do we say to those  individuals who have been on the front  lines  how do we help them how do we give them  assistance with still taking into  consideration that this stigma applies  to all of us yeah and  in in our house of medicine this stigma  is substantial  and i think you and i probably can both  recall days from our training  when it was very clear that strength was  associated with things like  how many hours you could stay up how  many patients you could admit  how many papers you could recall during  morning report  or ultimately how many papers you  published but  you know when was the last time that we  heard about a doctor  or a nurse being promoted for their  compassionate and empathetic care  of a patient you don't see that kind of  movement on the academic ladder often  enough  and so i think part of what we have to  do in the profession is recognize that  we've got to shift our culture so  that people who are struggling in our  profession can actually step forward  can get help my primary identity is  still that of  of a doctor and i think about our  colleagues in medicine  doctors nurses pharmacists and others  who have been on the front lines  providing care  who were already struggling before the  pandemic and i worry about the burnout  that we are seeing increase in that  population  because the truth is we need to serve  those who have been serving us we got to  take care  of our of our health care providers we  also know  that if they burn out at the rates that  we're seeing now we are not only going  to have a problem if there's a  resurgence in coping we will have  problems caring for people  for routine uh medical issues as well  you also fall into this category into  this group  right you're a physician uh you  experience burnout i'm sure just like  every other human  what's your strategy for battling that  that's a really good question and you  know i'm  i'm a work in progress you know i have  my days where i struggle as well  with burnout and with exhaustion and  and it's tough you know it goes in  cycles but there are a few things that i  found are very important to me  uh you know one is to make sure that i'm  anchoring myself  in relationships uh relationships in  particular with friends and family  you know when you get burned out  ironically the what gets pushed aside  often are your relationships  as you start to you know feel like you  have less energy interact with other  people to reach out to other people  uh you might feel more grumpy or or or  crabby around them as i often do  and that actually can push uh people  away but making sure for me that i'm  spending some time each day even if it's  just 15 minutes  uh talking to people i care about ask  them how they're doing  uh listening to them that's an important  source of healing the second thing that  i've  i've got to do is is to make sure that i  am also  pacing myself that i'm taking breaks  when i can and during those breaks that  i'm doing something that renews me and  finally one last thing which is  gratitude which i find is an  extraordinarily  powerful renewing source of energy  i try to keep a few moments at the end  of each day to just remember  what i'm grateful for i was honestly not  very good at doing that until i had kids  and because of my wife who is far more  resilient  and thoughtful than i am she began doing  this exercise with our children at the  end of each day  asking them what they were grateful for  and that's actually inspired me to do  that and it's been a really powerful  way to reconnect with meaning and to  feel renewed  after a long difficult day absolutely  i'm so glad you have that because  we have this age of information overload  constant anxiety whether it's from our  jobs our phones our family members  our friends and we start losing focus of  what brings us true fulfillment which is  the extrinsic factors you mentioned  these deep relationships meaning  gratitude uh even looking forward to the  future even it's for the next day for  something special  um the pandemic has really taken that  away from us you know for me the way  that i would cope with some of my  stressors pre-pandemic  was looking forward to the future so  when i was doing my residency working a  long icu shift i would say  oh next month i have less days that i'm  working so i would look forward to that  pandemic took that away another thing  that it took away was the relationships  you just mentioned  and it's created true loneliness even  coming out of the pandemic now as  some of the lockdowns ease in the united  states we're still experiencing  loneliness and i know that's a subject  that's really important to you as well  what can we do if we are having some  layers of anxiety  in sort of coming together again this is  such an important uh  point and this is one of the great costs  of the pandemic that's invisible  the loneliness and isolation that's  resulted from it it's very  understandable some people will feel  anxious  we're all going to move at different  paces and so the first thing is just to  recognize that  to not judge yourself but also not judge  others if they're going at a different  pace than you are  but the other piece is just to remember  here is that  if we've learned one thing from this  pandemic it's that our relationships  really do matter  they are how we get through tough times  they're also what sustains us  during times of stress and so in the way  that makes  most sense to you that feels most  comfortable for you just start reaching  out  to friends maybe it's a phone call maybe  it's a video call  maybe it's an outdoor walk if that's  what you feel more comfortable with or  maybe it's an indoor meal  that you share together like old times  but building that into your  into your days and into your weeks is  incredibly important because  all of us have been through trauma  together we may have experienced this  pandemic in different ways but we've all  experienced it deeply  and trauma requires healing one of the  most powerful source of healing that we  have  are our relationships with one another  and finally just remember  that means also that you can be a  powerful source of healing in someone  else's life  you don't need to have an md behind your  name or an rn  to be a healer you just need to be able  to show up in someone's life  to listen openly to them to be fully  present  and through that you can remind them  that they matter  that they're seen that they're  understood and that is where the real  healing begins  you have uh seven and a half million or  just under seven and a half million  viewers watching you subscribers  watching you  what's the message you want to leave  them off about their health or perhaps  about your second term as surgeon  general well  first i just want to thank everyone for  for tuning in and i just want to say  look at this moment where we're all  struggling through this pandemic  one of the most important things that  you can do to protect yourself protect  your family  is to get vaccinated and to make sure  that people around you  are get vaccinated too it is our  clearest path to getting back to our way  of life  and beyond this look we do have big  challenges we've got to take on  maybe you felt depressed maybe you're  feeling anxious about returning  uh to a post-pandemic life this is  something that's not  uncommon and so if you're feeling any of  these these things i want you to know  that you're not alone  once you know it doesn't mean that  you're broken in some way what it means  is that you're a human being having a  very  human experience  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you

